.\" Historical-source decision: House binary-patch specification, unrelated to historical text patch(1).
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH BPATCH 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
bpatch \- patch a binary from a checked specification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bpatch
.I spec target
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read a specification and an existing target file, apply the edits in
memory, then write the target back without changing its length.
All offsets and byte values are hexadecimal, without a radix prefix.
The specification accepts high-bit maux text or low-ASCII text.
Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored.
.TP
.B set offset byte ...
Replace successive bytes starting at offset.
.TP
.B expect offset byte
Require the byte at offset to have this value. This observes earlier edits
in the same specification.
.TP
.B flip-lc
Scan for absolute LDA, BIT and STA instructions whose operand is $C081 or
$C082, and change the low operand byte to $83 or $80 respectively.
This is a specialised language-card patch for compatible proshim tenants.
.PP
For example, a specification containing:
.PP
.B expect 0010 EA
.br
.B set 0010 60
.PP
replaces a checked NOP byte by RTS.
Keep an unmodified copy of the target.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/BPATCH  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dd(1), proshim(1), patch(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for a specification, range, expectation or I/O failure, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
The target is limited to 28672 bytes and the specification to 4096 bytes. Parse/expect failures occur before writing; a write failure may leave partial changes. flip-lc is a byte-pattern scan, not a disassembler, and can also alter data matching the pattern. Available on its development image.
